          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which of the following clinical findings most strongly suggests a secondary rath</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which of the following clinical findings most strongly suggests a secondary rather than primary cause of hypertension?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Family history of hypertension in both parents</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A strong family history supports primary (essential) hypertension, which has polygenic inheritance and often runs in families.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Gradual onset of hypertension at age 58</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Gradual onset in a middle-aged or older adult is the typical pattern of primary hypertension. Age under 40 without family history would be the red flag.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Spontaneous hypokalaemia without diuretic use</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Unprovoked low potassium suggests excess aldosterone production, pointing toward primary aldosteronism — one of the most important treatable secondary causes.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Body mass index of 34 kg/m²</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Obesity is a well-known risk factor for primary hypertension. While it predisposes to obstructive sleep apnoea (a secondary cause), a high BMI alone is not a specific red flag.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Red flags for secondary hypertension include: age <40 without family history, resistance to ≥3 drugs, spontaneous hypokalaemia, episodic headache-palpitations-sweating, and arm–leg BP discrepancy."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Spontaneous hypokalaemia is a key red flag for primary aldosteronism, one of the most common and curable secondary causes."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
