          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A patient presents with blood pressure of 210/130 mmHg. Neurological examination</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with blood pressure of 210/130 mmHg. Neurological examination is normal, fundoscopy shows no papilloedema, and serum troponin is negative. What is the most appropriate management?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reduce blood pressure gradually with oral agents over 24–48 hours</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Without acute end-organ damage, this is hypertensive urgency. Gradual oral reduction over 24–48 hours is safe and avoids the cerebral hypoperfusion risk of rapid lowering.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Start intravenous labetalol and arrange ICU admission</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Intravenous therapy and ICU admission are reserved for hypertensive emergencies, where acute end-organ damage is present. This patient has no evidence of such damage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Administer sublingual nifedipine for rapid blood pressure reduction</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Sublingual nifedipine causes unpredictable, precipitous blood pressure drops and risks cerebral or myocardial ischaemia. It should never be used in hypertensive crises.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Reassure and discharge without treatment</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A blood pressure of 210/130 mmHg always requires treatment. Even without acute organ damage, controlled oral reduction and close follow-up are essential.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "In hypertensive crisis (>180/120), check for acute end-organ damage: if absent → urgency (oral agents, 24–48 h); if present → emergency (IV therapy, ICU). Never use sublingual nifedipine."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The defining feature is the presence or absence of acute end-organ damage — not the blood pressure number itself."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
                "enableRetry": true,
                "enableSolutionsButton": true,
                "enableCheckButton": true,
