          "pause": true,
          "displayType": "poster",
          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A hypertensive patient's 12-lead ECG meets neither Sokolow-Lyon nor Cornell volt</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A hypertensive patient's 12-lead ECG meets neither Sokolow-Lyon nor Cornell voltage criteria for left ventricular hypertrophy. What is the most appropriate interpretation?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Cardiac target organ damage has been reliably excluded</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ECG voltage criteria detect only about 50% of anatomical left ventricular hypertrophy confirmed on echocardiography. A normal ECG does not exclude cardiac damage.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Echocardiography should be requested if clinical suspicion remains high</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Because ECG voltage criteria have limited sensitivity (~50%), echocardiography is the appropriate next step when clinical suspicion of left ventricular hypertrophy persists despite a normal ECG.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The ECG should be repeated annually as a screening measure</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A repeat ECG will have the same sensitivity limitation. If LVH is suspected now, the appropriate escalation is echocardiography, not a delayed re-screen.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The Sokolow-Lyon criterion alone is sufficient to confirm or exclude LVH</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>No single criterion is sufficient. Sokolow-Lyon has a sensitivity of only about 50%. Using both Sokolow-Lyon and Cornell improves detection but still misses a substantial proportion of cases.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Sokolow-Lyon (S in V1 + R in V5/V6 ≥ 35 mm) detects only ~50% of anatomical LVH. When clinical suspicion is high and ECG is negative, escalate to echocardiography."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. ECG voltage criteria are specific but not sensitive — a normal ECG never rules out LVH when clinical concern exists."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
