          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>A 62-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and proteinuria requires antihypertensi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 62-year-old woman with type 2 diabetes and proteinuria requires antihypertensive pharmacotherapy. Which first-line drug class is most appropriate?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Thiazide-like diuretic (e.g., chlorthalidone)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Thiazides are first-line for uncomplicated hypertension, but in diabetes with proteinuria, drugs that reduce intraglomerular pressure and slow renal progression — ACE inhibitors or ARBs — take priority.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Beta-blocker (e.g., metoprolol)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Beta-blockers are reserved for heart failure or post-myocardial infarction, not uncomplicated hypertension or diabetic nephropathy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Calcium channel blocker (e.g., amlodipine)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Calcium channel blockers suit elderly patients and isolated systolic hypertension, but they lack the renoprotective benefit that ACE inhibitors and ARBs provide in proteinuric kidney disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>ACE inhibitor or angiotensin receptor blocker</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>ACE inhibitors and ARBs are the first choice in diabetes with proteinuria. They reduce intraglomerular pressure and slow renal disease progression beyond their blood-pressure-lowering effect.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Match the drug to the compelling indication: ACEi/ARB for diabetes with proteinuria or CKD; CCB for elderly/ISH; thiazide for uncomplicated HTN; beta-blocker for heart failure or post-MI."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. In diabetes with proteinuria, RAAS blockade with an ACE inhibitor or ARB is the compelling first choice."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
